---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import StepsStrip from '../components/StepsStrip.astro';

const faq = [
  {
    q: 'I have forgotten the password. Can this page open my file?',
    a: 'No, and no page can. If a PDF asks for a password before it will open, that password has to be known and typed. A page that promises to open such a file without it is describing the other kind of password (the permission switches) while letting you believe it means this one. We do not write that promise, here or anywhere.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How can the permission kind come off without typing anything? Is that breaking in?',
    a: 'No. A permissions password does not lock the content away: the file already opens, and the restrictions are switches a reader agrees to honour. Taking them off is re-saving the file without the switches. Every serious PDF tool does it, and the PDF standard itself names it as a separate thing from opening a locked file.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Where does my password go?',
    a: 'Nowhere. It is typed into this page and used inside this page, in your browser, and it is gone when the page closes. The check-it-yourself page lets you watch the network while a job runs, this one included.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Will my form answers, attachments and pages survive?',
    a: 'Yes, in our own testing: this tool was checked against files protected with every standard password kind, including a filled form, a file with attachments, a file carrying a signature, and a 300-page file. Pages, form answers and attachments all came through whole, and the finished file was re-opened and counted before the download was offered.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What happens to a signed PDF?',
    a: 'The signature is carried over with the file, but any re-save, here or in any tool, means the signed bytes are no longer the exact bytes that were signed, so the signature will no longer verify as untouched. If a signature must stay provable, keep the protected original as the record and treat the unlocked copy as a working copy.',
  },
];
const faqJson = JSON.stringify({
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: faq.map((f) => ({ '@type': 'Question', name: f.q, acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: f.a } })),
});
const howToJson = JSON.stringify({
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'HowTo',
  name: "How to take a password off your own PDF",
  step: [
    { '@type': 'HowToStep', position: 1, name: "Pick the PDF", text: "Drop a PDF on the tray, or click to pick one. It stays on this device. The page checks what kind of password the file has: one that locks it shut, or one that only switches off printing, copying and editing." },
    { '@type': 'HowToStep', position: 2, name: "Type the open password, if the file asks for one", text: "If the file asks for a password to open, type that password: it must be the right one. If the file opens already, leave the box empty; its permission switches come off without one." },
    { '@type': 'HowToStep', position: 3, name: "Take the password off", text: "Press 'Take the password off'. The file is re-saved in your browser with no password, then checked: it must open with no password and carry every page." },
    { '@type': 'HowToStep', position: 4, name: "Download the unlocked PDF", text: "Take the finished file away. Your protected original is never changed." },
  ],
});
---

<Base title="Unlock PDF: take a password off your own PDF, free · Benchzy" description="Take the password off a PDF you own, inside your browser. An open password must be known and typed; a permissions password on a file that already opens comes off without one. Your file and password never leave this page.">
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-5xl px-4">
    <section class="mt-8">
      <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-accent-deep">PDF tools · passwords</p>
      <h1 class="font-display text-4xl font-black mt-2">Unlock PDF</h1>
      <p class="mt-3 text-lg leading-relaxed max-w-2xl">
        Take the password off a PDF that is yours, so it opens like an ordinary file
        again. The work runs in your browser; the file and the password never leave
        this page.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="ticket mt-6 p-5 sm:p-7" id="un-tool">
      <div id="un-tray" class="tray p-6 text-center cursor-pointer" role="button" tabindex="0" aria-label="Pick a PDF">
        <p class="font-display text-xl font-bold">Drop a PDF on the tray, or click to pick one</p>
        <p class="font-mono text-xs text-ink-soft mt-2">PDF · it stays on this device</p>
        <input id="un-file" type="file" accept="application/pdf,.pdf" class="hidden" />
      </div>
      <p class="file-loaded hidden" data-loaded-for="un-file" role="status"></p>

      <div class="grid gap-6 mt-6 md:grid-cols-[1fr_320px]">
        <div>
          <h2 class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-ink-soft">The password</h2>
          <label class="block mt-3 text-sm font-bold" for="un-pw">The file's open password</label>
          <input id="un-pw" type="password" class="mt-1 w-full border border-ink bg-ticket px-3 py-2" autocomplete="off" />
          <label class="mt-3 flex items-center gap-2 text-sm text-ink-soft cursor-pointer">
            <input id="un-show" type="checkbox" /> Show the password while I type
          </label>
          <p class="action-note">
            Leave the box empty if the file opens without one: that is the permission
            kind, and its switches come off with nothing typed. If the file asks for a
            password to open, type it exactly; a wrong one simply does not open the
            file, and the page will say so.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h2 class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-ink-soft">File spec</h2>
          <dl id="un-spec" class="mt-2">
            <div class="spec-row"><dt>file</dt><dd>none picked yet</dd></div>
          </dl>
          <button id="un-run" class="btn-primary w-full mt-5" type="button" disabled>Take the password off</button>
          <p id="un-status" class="font-mono text-sm mt-4 leading-relaxed" aria-live="polite">Pick a PDF to begin.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div id="un-result" class="receipt hidden mt-8">
        <div class="tear pt-5 flex items-center gap-4 flex-wrap">
          <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold">Result</h2>
          <span class="stamp" id="un-stamp">Done on your device</span>
        </div>
        <p id="un-achieved" class="mt-3 leading-relaxed max-w-2xl"></p>
        <a id="un-download" class="btn-primary inline-block no-underline mt-3" href="#" download>Download the unlocked PDF</a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="ticket-soft mt-6 p-5 sm:p-6" aria-label="The two kinds of PDF password">
      <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold">Two kinds of password. Read this first.</h2>
      <div class="mt-3 grid gap-5 md:grid-cols-2">
        <div>
          <h3 class="font-bold">1. A password that locks the file shut</h3>
          <p class="text-ink-soft leading-relaxed mt-1">
            If a PDF asks for a password before it will open, that password must be
            known and typed here. There is no other way, on this site or any other.
            If you have forgotten it, this page cannot help, and neither can anyone
            else.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3 class="font-bold">2. A password that only switches things off</h3>
          <p class="text-ink-soft leading-relaxed mt-1">
            Some PDFs open fine but will not print, copy or edit. That kind is a set
            of permission switches, and it can be taken off without typing it,
            because that is how PDF permissions work in every tool. It is not a
            trick, and it is not breaking in.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed">
        <strong>Your files only:</strong> only take passwords off your own files, or
        files you have permission to change. You are responsible for that right.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-2 leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
        The password is typed into this page and used inside this page. It is never
        sent anywhere, and it is gone when the page closes. The
        <a class="underline underline-offset-4" href="/check-it-yourself/">check-it-yourself page</a>
        lets you watch that happen, like every other job here.
      </p>
    </section>

    <StepsStrip who="For your own PDF whose password you know, or whose print, copy and edit switches you want back." steps={[{ n: '01', t: 'Pick the PDF' }, { n: '02', t: 'Type the password, if it asks for one' }, { n: '03', t: 'Take it off and download' }]} />

    <section class="mt-12 grid gap-8 md:grid-cols-2">
      <div>
        <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold border-b-2 border-ink pb-2">How it works</h2>
        <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
          The page opens the file with the password (or with the empty password, for
          the permission kind), then re-saves it with the protection left out
          entirely: not just the password prompt, but the old protection machinery
          itself, so the finished file carries no trace of it. The result is checked
          before download: it must open with no password and hold every page the
          original held. Your protected original is never changed.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold border-b-2 border-ink pb-2">Honest limits</h2>
        <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
          An open password that is not known cannot be helped, by this page or any
          other; that is the whole point of the lock. A signed file keeps its
          signature through the re-save, but the signature will no longer verify as
          untouched, because the bytes changed; keep the original as the record.
          Files whose insides this page cannot read are refused in the open, never
          guessed at.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-10 mb-4">
      <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold border-b-2 border-ink pb-2">Questions</h2>
      <div class="mt-4 grid gap-4">
        {faq.map((f) => (
          <article class="ticket-soft p-5">
            <h3 class="font-bold">{f.q}</h3>
            <p class="text-ink-soft leading-relaxed mt-1">{f.a}</p>
          </article>
        ))}
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={faqJson}></script>
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={howToJson}></script>
  <script>
    import '../scripts/unlock-pdf.js';
  </script>
</Base>
